From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FramePlayer contributors <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 23:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] xr: controller vibration through OpenXR haptics

WebXR pages vibrate controllers with
inputSource.gamepad.vibrationActuator.playEffect("dual-rumble", ...), as
on Meta Quest's browser. Chromium returned no actuator for XR input
sources (crbug.com/955097) and its OpenXR device had no haptic output.

- Blink: GamepadHapticActuator can play through a Delegate instead of the
  gamepad service. XRInputSource is that delegate for immersive sessions:
  each controller's gamepad gets a "dual-rumble" actuator that plays the
  stronger of the two magnitudes for the effect's duration (after its
  start delay), resolves "complete" when the duration is over, and
  "preempted" when another effect or reset() replaces it.
- XRPresentationProvider.ApplyHapticFeedback(source_id, amplitude,
  duration) carries it to the device; zero amplitude or duration stops.
- OpenXR: one vibration output action per hand, bound to
  /user/hand/{left,right}/output/haptic for every controller profile that
  has it (not the hand-tracking profiles), played with
  xrApplyHapticFeedback (unspecified frequency) and stopped with
  xrStopHapticFeedback. --vmodule=openxr_input_helper=1 logs each result.
- ARCore and Cardboard ignore it; the fake OpenXR runtime accepts
  vibration actions.

Applies on top of 0006 (and IWFDK's 0004).

Bug: 955097
